WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.600
Like many Disney movies, Avengers: Endgame
threads the needle between age groups, trying

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.180
to offer a solid story that youngsters and
adults can appreciate, but also adding enough

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:14.210
mom-and-pop jokes to keep Mom and Pop engaged.
Avengers: Endgame had plenty of things that

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:16.860
went right over kids' heads. Spoilers ahead.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.800
After the five-year time jump early in the
film, we see Captain America leading a grief

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:26.259
counseling group meant to help people move
on after Thanos' snap. With half the living

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:29.940
creatures in the universe gone, there are
a lot of issues left unresolved.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:34.320
All the surviving Avengers are struggling
to move on after the weight of a crushing,

00:00:34.329 --> 00:00:39.440
genocidal loss that's beyond alien to most
kids. Adults, on the other hand, are more

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:44.390
likely to key into what are obviously some
largely unhealthy coping mechanisms for the

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:49.260
Avengers' loss. Of them all, Tony is the closest
to moving on in a healthy way, but he's done

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:53.710
so by moving as far into the country as he
can so as to never engage with the wreckage

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:58.660
of his biggest heartbreak. He's deep in denial,
to the point where he kicks the Avengers out

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:01.540
when they try to convince him there's a chance
to make things right.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:06.040
It wouldn't be an exaggeration to say that
both Avengers: Infinity War and Avengers:

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:11.470
Endgame are built on father issues. You've
got the ultimate abusive big dad in Thanos,

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:15.340
who tells everyone he meets that his genocidal
warpath is all for the good of the people

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:19.690
he's killing; that he's actually being the
good guy by doing the bad thing for the right

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:25.200
reasons. Then you've got Tony Stark, who struggles
with being a father figure for Peter Parker,

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.710
and for whom the latter's death weighs heavily
enough in Endgame to lead Tony to undertake

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:34.580
a mission through time to bring Spider-Man
back. Then there's Thor, who often struggles

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:37.110
under the weight of his own father's expectations.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.820
Basically, a lot of the Avengers are working
through their dad issues, which is crystallized

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.570
in a long moment of Tony talking to his father,
Howard Stark, while on a mission through time

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:51.640
in the 1970s. Tony manages to have a pretty
in-depth heart-to-heart with his old man,

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:56.490
even though Howard has no idea that he's actually
talking to his son. Tony finally gets to work

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:00.220
through some of his feelings and admit that
Howard was a pretty good dad underneath it

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:04.380
all. It's the kind of perspective that you're
not likely to get until you're well on your

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.090
way to becoming a dad yourself.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:13.360
"What is and always will be my greatest creation
is you."

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.920
When Thor and Rocket go back to Asgard to
get the Reality Stone, the God of Thunder

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:23.620
pretty immediately gets sidetracked when he
sees his mother, Frigga. Thor remembers that

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:28.170
the day they're visiting Asgard is the same
day his mother dies in the past, which brings

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:33.040
up some understandably complicated emotions
for him. Frigga finds him, however, and they're

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.510
able to have one last loaded conversation
about family, the future, and the burden of

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:38.360
expectations.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.640
It's the kind of perfect last conversation
that many adults wish they could have had

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.210
with their parents after they've passed. That
level of melancholic fantasy isn't common

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:51.650
for most kids, thankfully, and the opportunity
for Thor to have one last chat with his doomed

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:56.750
mother likely put more than a few adults in
the audience to tears. Real people don't have

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:01.100
the ability to go back in time and get closure
with their parents, but plenty have dreamed

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:01.860
of the chance.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:07.040
While Captain America has been leading therapy
sessions, and Natasha has been leading superhero

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:11.860
teams, Thor has been leading New Asgard…
at least in terms of alcohol consumption.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:15.690
The massive amount of alcohol Thor's been
drinking has given the God of Thunder a bit

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:20.000
of a beer gut, which leads to a handful of
fat jokes throughout the rest of the movie.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:24.640
He's clearly dealing with depression and grief,
and it's led him into a destructive lifestyle

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:29.600
that's radically changed his body. Kids may
or may not recognize that Thor's newly rotund

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:33.680
figure is a product of beer drinking, and
they certainly aren't going to clue into Thor's

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.460
decision to look for a way out of the emptiness
of his life at the bottom of a bottle.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:42.900
When it comes to time travel in movies, things
almost never make sense for very long. Most

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:46.790
of the time, these movies just kind of ask
you to accept the basic concept and ignore

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:51.220
anything weird that happens later. Endgame,
however, spends a lot of time calling out

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:56.630
various misconceptions about how time travel
works in the MCU. Movies get involved when

00:03:56.630 --> 00:04:01.210
Rhodey and Scott Lang list off some of their
time travel favorites: Back to the Future,

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:06.040
Hot Tub Time Machine, Bill &amp; Ted's Excellent
Adventure, Star Trek, Quantum Leap, Time Cop,

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:06.800
and The Terminator.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:11.120
Most kids aren't going to have seen more than
a couple of movies on the list, and they rattle

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:15.430
them off so quickly that even adults might
have trouble keeping up. Still, even if you

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:19.470
were quick enough to recognize the films and
remember each one's specific rules regarding

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:24.400
time travel, it doesn't matter. Hulk pretty
quickly shuts Rhodey and Scott down immediately

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.280
by telling them that they're all wrong.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:31.440
After Scott finally escapes the Quantum Realm
thanks to a helpful rat, he has to alert a

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.160
security guard to let him and his Quantum
Realm doohickeys out of the storage space

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:40.220
they're being held in. That security guard
is played by none other than Ken Jeong, who's

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:43.300
previously worked with the Russo brothers
on the series Community.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:52.120
"And a one, a two, a Chang Chang Chang four."

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:56.140
The cameo alone might be enough to mention
here, but there's one more detail in that

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:57.740
scene that's just for adults.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:02.860
The security guard is reading J. G. Ballard's
The Terminal Beach, a collection of science-fiction

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:07.490
short stories. One of the most famous stories
in The Terminal Beach is called "End-Game."

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:12.080
It doesn't bear many similarities to the film,
but the shared title is certainly no coincidence.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:16.760
If you thought there was something vaguely
familiar about Thor's hair, beard, and sunglasses,

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:21.480
you weren't the only one. Tony Stark noticed,
too. For a good chunk of the film, it seems

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:26.000
like Chris Hemsworth's Thor is taking style
lessons from Jeff Bridges' The Dude, of Big

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:26.860
Lebowski fame.

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:30.380
"I'm the Dude, so that's what you call me,
you know?"

00:05:30.389 --> 00:05:34.940
The R-rated cult classic from 1998 isn't a
movie that most kids are going to be familiar

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:38.340
with, but Endgame isn't content to just leave
it as a visual gag.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:43.840
When Stark calls Thor "Lebowski" in passing,
it takes the quick gag into meta territory.

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:49.600
Bridges played both the Dude and also Obadiah
Stane way back in Iron Man. Stane was Tony's

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:54.870
business partner and, eventually, his supervillainous
rival as Iron Monger. That Tony is the one

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:59.479
to call Thor out on his resemblance to one
of Bridges' less-villainous roles is icing

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:02.180
on a cake that's just for the adults in the
audience.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:06.480
Just because it's a serious mission doesn't
mean that there's not a bit of room for levity.

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:11.240
Ant Man and Iron Man certainly take that to
heart when they journey back to 2012 along

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:15.520
with Captain America in order to get some
of the Infinity Stones. Both Tony and Scott

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.010
take a moment to critique the past version
of Captain America's hindquarters in his Avengers-era

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:24.980
get-up. Ultimately, Scott proclaims it to
be "America's ass," which is hard to argue

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:25.480
with.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.040
Later in the film, when present Cap is fighting
past Cap, he takes a moment after the fight

00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:35.120
to admit that he really does have America's
best butt. It's clear in just about every

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.860
scene, but that rearward-looking dialogue
is likely to fly right over kids' heads, albeit

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:43.300
with some giggling that their favorite heroes
just said a mild cuss word. Meanwhile, the

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:47.880
adults in the audience are likely to feel
a bit called out for staring.

